---
.title = "Native UI (experimental)",
.description = "Your HTML, CSS and JavaScript drawn with native views instead of a WebView: no browser process, a fraction of the memory, the same web code.",
.date = .date("2026-10-04T00:00:00"),
.authors = ["Oriel"],
.layout = "docs.shtml",
---

An Oriel app normally shows its page in the system's webview. The native renderer is an optional second renderer: the app's own `index.html`, `style.css` and `app.js` run in a small JavaScript engine inside the app, and the page is drawn with the platform's views. There is no browser and no renderer process.

It is **experimental**: it runs on Linux (GTK 4), Windows (Direct2D), macOS (AppKit), iOS (UIKit) and Android, behind a build flag, and it covers the HTML and CSS that map onto native views, not the whole web platform. The [showcase](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/tree/main/examples/showcase) runs on it unchanged: dictation, chat with a local model, notes in SQLite, files, notifications.

[](<$image.siteAsset('screenshots/native-showcase.png').alt('The showcase drawn by the native renderer on Linux')>)

*The showcase on the native renderer (GTK 4): no WebView, the same `index.html`, `style.css` and `app.js`.*

# Renderer features

The optional `-Dnative_ui` path supports Linux, Windows, macOS, Android and iOS. Features include Android WebView-sized controls and fractional text metrics, background frame suspension, sliders and Zig build-option forwarding in the mobile CLI. The renderer also supports inline text and SVG styling, input events, canvas drawing and renderer benchmarking tools.

Canvas recording writes its numeric program directly, while transform and opacity updates travel as numbers without flattening the page again. The render bench can also measure power on Android and Linux where battery or RAPL readings are available (see its [measurement instructions](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/tree/main/examples/render-bench#power)). QuickJS enforces the app's CSP for `eval`, Function constructors and string timers.

The renderer remains experimental. The benchmark tables below retain their measurement dates and conditions; they are not new measurements of every subsequent commit. The latest Android emulator run reached about 60 fps in the built-in workloads; no Android before/after comparison or battery saving has been established.

The page's DOM is Oriel's own: a **native DOM**, a document store written in Zig behind QuickJS bindings, replaced the JavaScript DOM library ([linkedom](https://github.com/WebReflection/linkedom)) the renderer started with. It builds a page about twice as fast as linkedom did and keeps memory flat (below).

# Why

A webview is a browser. On Android, every WebView app starts Chromium's renderer in a sandboxed process of its own, and the app process carries Chromium's code too. For a small app that is most of the memory it uses.

```=html
