<figcaption>Down each column: what you write, the JavaScript engine, the document, styles and layout, drawing, and the native code. The line on the right is how the page reaches native code: IPC messages across processes for the WebView, a direct call in the same process for the native renderer, JSI for React Native.</figcaption>
</figure>
```

| | Oriel WebView | Oriel native | React Native |
|---|---|---|---|
| You write | HTML, CSS, JS (any framework) | The same files | React components (JSX) |
| JavaScript engine | The system browser's, with a JIT | QuickJS, ahead-of-time bytecode | Hermes, ahead-of-time bytecode |
| The document | The browser's DOM | The native DOM: a Zig store, thin JS wrappers | React's tree, then Fabric's C++ shadow tree |
| Styles and layout | The whole of CSS | The CSS app interfaces use, laid out by Yoga | Style props, laid out by Yoga |
| What draws | The browser's compositor | One platform view draws the page; text fields, selects and sliders are real platform widgets | One platform view per component |
| Processes | Two: the app and a sandboxed renderer | One | One |
| Calling native code | IPC messages, serialized | A direct call on the same thread | JSI, a direct call |
| A text change reaches the screen | Style, layout and paint in the browser | One call into the tree; layout skipped when only that text's width changes | A React render, a Fabric commit, a mount on the UI thread |

The native renderer keeps the WebView's way of writing an app and React Native's footprint: no second process, no browser engine, the page's own files compiled ahead of time. Where React Native makes a platform view for every component, Oriel draws the page with one view and its own layout, so a list of a thousand rows is a thousand records in the tree, not a thousand Android or UIKit views.

# Speed

The [render bench](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/tree/main/examples/render-bench) times one page in both renderers: building and updating rows of styled text (each followed by a layout read, as `offsetHeight` forces in a browser), a `requestAnimationFrame` loop moving 200 boxes, and a `<canvas>` game loop.

The same bench running in each renderer on a Linux desktop with a 180 Hz display (two recordings of one run each, at the same speed):

```=html
<div class="nr-videos">
<figure><figcaption>Native renderer</figcaption><video src="/Oriel/videos/render-bench-native.mp4" muted loop controls preload="metadata"></video></figure>
<figure><figcaption>WebView (WebKitGTK)</figcaption><video src="/Oriel/videos/render-bench-webview.mp4" muted loop controls preload="metadata"></video></figure>
</div>
```


Linux desktop with a 180 Hz display, ReleaseFast, the median of 3 runs (2026-10-03):

| | Native renderer | WebView (WebKitGTK) |
|---|---|---|
| Build 1000 / 3000 rows | **6.4 / 18.6 ms** | 20 / 69 ms |
| Update 1000 / 3000 rows | **2.8 / 8.5 ms** | 9 / 40 ms |
| Page script runs | **19 ms** after start | 349 ms |
| First frame | **69 ms** | 431 ms |
| Animate 200 boxes | **165 fps** | 62 fps |
| Canvas, 200 / 1000 balls | **180 / 91 fps** (the display's rate is 180) | 62 / 62 fps |
| Memory at start | **110 MB** | 491 MB |
| Memory after the tests | **174 MB**, 177 after a second round | 561 MB |

The native renderer beats the WebView on every test: it runs the page's script about eighteen times sooner and shows its first frame six times sooner, builds rows three times faster and updates them three to five times faster, animates at the display's refresh rate (the WebView stays at 60), and uses about a third of the memory.

What got it there, in order: an incremental render (only what changed is restyled and flattened), text changes sent straight to the native node, a QuickJS fix (its hash tables were leaving most buckets empty), the renderer's and the app's JavaScript compiled to bytecode at build time, the native DOM (below), transform-only animation frames, `requestAnimationFrame` paced by the display, rows and whole lists of identical rows built by the tree in Zig straight from the native DOM, smaller nodes from pools, text sized from cached glyph widths, a text change that only moves its own edge skipping the layout, and style sheets parsed when the app is built.

## On every platform

The same bench, the native renderer, ReleaseFast (Windows: ReleaseSafe, as `oriel build` makes it), the median of 3:

| | Build 1000 rows | Update 1000 rows | Animate 200 boxes | Canvas 1000 balls |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Linux (desktop, 180 Hz) | 6.4 ms | 2.8 ms | 165 fps | 91 fps |
| Windows 11 (a 2018 laptop, 144 Hz) | 18.9 ms | 8.2 ms | 72 fps | 64 fps |
| macOS (M1, 120 Hz display) | 24.9 ms | 15.6 ms | 120 fps | 120 fps |
| iOS (simulator, 60 Hz) | 22.3 ms | 13.8 ms | 60 fps | 60 fps |
| Android (a 120 Hz phone) | 13.8 ms | 18.9 ms | 116 fps | 91 fps |

Where a test reaches the display's refresh rate, that is its ceiling.

A canvas can also be drawn from the app's Zig code (`oriel.canvas`): the page keeps its HTML and CSS, and the drawing calls skip the JavaScript engine. With 1000 balls drawn from Zig, every platform reaches its display's rate: 179 fps on Linux (91 from JavaScript), 143 on Windows (64), 120 on macOS and on the phone (91 from JavaScript there). The [Breakout example](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/tree/main/examples/breakout) runs the same game both ways, and in the WebView. At 500 balls:

| | Native, Zig | Native, JavaScript | WebView |
|---|---|---|---|
| Windows 11 (a 2018 laptop, 144 Hz) | 142–144 fps | 59–75 fps | 103–116 fps |
| Android (a 120 Hz phone) | 120 fps, 0.35 ms a frame | 63 fps, 6.6 ms a frame | 120 fps, 1.2 ms a frame |

The browsers' JavaScript engines run the game's own code faster than QuickJS does; moved to Zig, the same game leaves them behind.

[](<$image.siteAsset('screenshots/breakout-desktop.png').alt('Breakout in the native renderer on Linux, Zig mode: bricks, balls and the stats overlay')>)

## The native DOM

The renderer started on [linkedom](https://github.com/WebReflection/linkedom), a DOM written in JavaScript. Every element, attribute and text node was a JavaScript object, and building a page meant allocating thousands of them in the interpreter. The native DOM keeps the document in a Zig store instead: nodes are records in slabs, ids and classes are interned, selectors are matched natively, and JavaScript only holds small wrappers for the nodes it touches.

```=html
<figure class="nr-fig">
<svg viewBox="0 0 760 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="nr-dom-t">
