    <text class="nr-v" x="408" y="53">40</text>
    <text class="nr-s" x="200" y="80" text-anchor="end">linkedom</text>
    <rect class="nr-bar" x="212" y="66" width="400" height="20" rx="4"/>
    <text class="nr-v" x="620" y="81">85</text>
    <text class="nr-h" x="20" y="116">Update 1000 rows (ms)</text>
    <text class="nr-s" x="200" y="144" text-anchor="end">native DOM</text>
    <rect class="nr-bar nr-acc-fill" x="212" y="130" width="229" height="20" rx="4"/>
    <text class="nr-v" x="449" y="145">5.6</text>
    <text class="nr-s" x="200" y="172" text-anchor="end">linkedom</text>
    <rect class="nr-bar" x="212" y="158" width="400" height="20" rx="4"/>
    <text class="nr-v" x="620" y="173">9.8</text>
    <text class="nr-h" x="20" y="208">Memory after the bench (MB)</text>
    <text class="nr-s" x="200" y="236" text-anchor="end">native DOM</text>
    <rect class="nr-bar nr-acc-fill" x="212" y="222" width="334" height="20" rx="4"/>
    <text class="nr-v" x="554" y="237">127</text>
    <text class="nr-s" x="200" y="264" text-anchor="end">linkedom</text>
    <rect class="nr-bar" x="212" y="250" width="400" height="20" rx="4"/>
    <text class="nr-v" x="620" y="265">152</text>
  </g>
</svg>
<figcaption>Linux desktop. linkedom's memory kept growing round after round; the native DOM's stays flat.</figcaption>
</figure>
```

Every platform got the same gain:

| Build 1000 rows | Native DOM | linkedom |
|---|---|---|
| Linux | 40 ms | 85 ms |
| macOS (Apple M1) | 62 ms | 104 ms |
| Windows (a 2018 laptop) | 128 ms | 223 ms |
| Android (a Chromebook) | 194 ms | 290 ms |

These are the native DOM's own gains, measured when it replaced linkedom; the work since then (rows stamped from the DOM) took Linux to the 9 ms above, and Android to 13 ms (below). The JavaScript heap of an idle page went from about 29 MB to about 2 MB. The native DOM is the default; `-Dnative_dom=false` builds the renderer on linkedom, to compare the two or as a fallback while a page needs something the native DOM lacks.

## On Android, against React Native and plain views

Same Chromebook (arm64, a 60 Hz display), the same bench written for each (release builds; React Native 0.87 with Fabric and Hermes), the median of 3:

| | Plain Android views | Oriel native | Oriel WebView | React Native |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Build 1000 rows | 54 ms | **13–14 ms** (35–37 on screen) | 13 ms | 475–514 ms |
| Build 3000 rows | 183 ms | **36–40 ms** (108–188 on screen) | 51 ms | 2284–2700 ms |
| Update 1000 rows | **4.7 ms** | 31 ms (37–38 on screen) | 6.4 ms | 127–161 ms |
| Update 3000 rows | **15 ms** | 74–78 ms (89–91 on screen) | 17.5 ms | 299–302 ms |
| Animate 200 boxes | 60 fps | 59 fps | 45 fps | 59 fps |
| Canvas, 200 / 1000 balls | 60 / 60 fps | 59 / 49 fps | 51 / 48 fps | 60 / 60 fps (Skia) |
| Memory after the tests | 48 MB | 109–114 MB | about 151 MB | 145–157 MB |

What is timed: plain views until measured and laid out; Oriel's page time until the layout read returns, which includes Android's view getting the change ("on screen" adds Android's layout pass and the draw); the WebView's layout inside Chromium; React Native until Fabric has mounted the views on the UI thread. Memory is every process the app runs (the WebView's sandboxed renderer included).

Oriel now builds rows faster than plain Android views: one view draws every row, where plain views make four Android views a row. Counting until the rows are on screen, it builds 1000 rows about 13 times faster than React Native mounts them, and updates them three to four times faster. Updates are where plain views and the WebView still lead: changing 1000 texts goes through JavaScript and the DOM in Oriel, and is a field write for plain views. Animation follows the display: the canvas demo runs at 118 fps on a 120 Hz phone.

# Your web stack, unchanged

The native renderer runs whatever your web build produces. It isn't a framework of its own and has no component API to learn: it gives the page a DOM, CSS and the usual browser APIs, so anything that renders through the DOM works.

| | Status |
|---|---|
| Plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript | The [showcase](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/tree/main/examples/showcase): every tab |
| React 19 | [GhostPen](https://github.com/highercomve/GhostPen) is a React app; controlled inputs, selects, checkboxes and sliders reach `onChange` (the runtime's tests run real React). Vite's React starter looks as in the WebView |
| Vue 3 | Vite's Vue starter, built with `vite build`: looks as in the WebView, and its counter updates on click |
| Svelte 5 | Vite's Svelte starter: looks as in the WebView, and its counter updates on click |
| Preact | Vite's Preact starter: looks as in the WebView, and its counter updates on click |
| CSS-in-JS (styled-components, emotion) and styles injected at runtime | React apps with each, and a Vue app whose scoped styles arrive from JavaScript: they look as in the WebView, before and after their props change |
| Alpine.js 3 | A counter, a list with add and remove, and `x-if`: as in the WebView. Alpine compiles its expressions with `new Function`, which Oriel's default CSP forbids, natively as in the WebView (an `EvalError`, the violation logged): allow it (`'unsafe-eval'` in `security.csp`'s `script-src`) or use Alpine's CSP build (`@alpinejs/csp`) |
| Anything else that renders through the DOM (Solid, htmx…) | Being tried |
| Web components (Lit) | Not yet: shadow DOM and constructed style sheets are missing |

Use your usual tools: Vite, esbuild or any bundler, TypeScript, JSX, ES modules and lazy chunks. Oriel compiles the built JavaScript to bytecode and parses the CSS when the app is built, so the same `dist/` folder runs in the WebView and the native renderer.

The limits are the renderer's, not the framework's: the CSS it supports (below), and browser APIs a page may use that aren't there yet (for example `iframe` and Blob URLs). A page that uses something missing still runs; that part is ignored. The WebView build stays available for anything that needs the whole web platform: the same app builds both ways, with or without `-Dnative_ui`.

# Try it

Add `-Dnative_ui` to the build. Nothing else changes: the same `main.zig`, commands, events and web files.

```sh
# Linux
zig build -Dnative_ui run

# Android
zig build -Dtarget=aarch64-linux-android -Doptimize=ReleaseSafe -Dnative_ui
(cd android && gradle assembleRelease)
```

Your app's `build.zig` passes the option to Oriel (the showcase does: `.native_ui = b.option(bool, "native_ui", …)`). With it, windows never create a WebView; without it, nothing of the renderer is compiled in. Add `-Dnative_dom=false` to run the page on linkedom instead of the native DOM.

The same flag works for every target: `-Dtarget=x86_64-windows`, macOS and iOS builds. `oriel android dev/build` and `oriel ios dev/build` forward Zig `-D` options (see [Building]($link.page('docs/building'))).

