```

**The page's JavaScript** runs in [QuickJS-ng](https://github.com/quickjs-ng/quickjs), a small embeddable engine compiled into the app; the renderer's own JavaScript is compiled to QuickJS bytecode at build time, so it doesn't parse at startup. The **native DOM** gives the page `document`, elements, `querySelector`, events, `classList`, `dataset`: the nodes live in a Zig store, and JavaScript sees them through thin wrappers. Oriel adds what a page expects from a browser: timers, `location.hash` and `history`, `matchMedia`, `localStorage`, `KeyboardEvent`, form submission, and `window.oriel`, the same bridge to your Zig commands and events as in the WebView. Your scripts run as `<script>` tags would, in order.

**The style engine** parses each stylesheet once and indexes its rules by their rightmost selector. For every element it runs the cascade (specificity, `!important`, inline styles), resolves custom properties and `calc()`, inherits text properties and evaluates `@media` (width, `prefers-color-scheme`, `pointer`).

**The flattener** turns the styled DOM into as few native nodes as it can (below). It is incremental: the DOM's mutations mark the elements that changed, and only those are restyled and flattened; elements with the same styles share one computed style. Only the differences cross over to Zig, as small operations: create a node, set its properties, set its children, remove it.

**Three faster paths** skip those operations for the commonest changes. A text that changes its words goes straight to its node (`host.text`). New nodes are made from a style defined once, instead of each carrying its properties (`host.leaf`). And a flex row of simple children, or a list of identical rows, is **stamped**: Zig reads the children straight from the native DOM and makes their nodes itself, with no JavaScript object per child. That is how 1000 rows build in 9 ms on Linux and 13 ms on Android.

**Animation** follows the display: `requestAnimationFrame` runs on the platform's frame clock (GTK's frame clock, CVDisplayLink and CADisplayLink, DWM, Android's Choreographer), so a 120 Hz screen gets 120 frames and a hidden window none. While transitions and animations run, only the animated nodes are sent each frame.

**Zig** keeps one node per native view, maps the CSS onto [Yoga](https://www.yogalayout.dev/), the flexbox engine React Native uses, and lays the tree out, with CSS's paint order (z-index, positioned and sticky boxes), tables, sticky headers and horizontal scrolling on top. Text is measured by the platform's own text engine, so a paragraph wraps where it will be drawn; a text's one-line size is kept on its node and sizes by content and width in a cache, so most layouts measure nothing new.

**The backend** draws. On every platform one view draws the whole page (boxes, gradients, borders, shadows, styled text, SVG icons) and the fields are real platform widgets placed over it: the system keyboard, selection, autocorrect and accessibility of a text field come for free.

## The parts

| Part | What it does | In the source |
|---|---|---|
| QuickJS-ng | Runs the page's JavaScript and Oriel's runtime, which is compiled to bytecode at build time | `src/native_ui/vendor/quickjs-ng`, `qjs_shim.c`, `tools/qjs_bytecode.c` |
| The runtime | What a page expects from a browser: timers, events, `requestAnimationFrame`, `location`, `localStorage`, `window.oriel` | `src/native_ui/js/src/main.js` |
| The native DOM | The document in a Zig store; `innerHTML` and selectors handled natively | `src/native_ui/dom` |
| Style engine | The cascade, custom properties, inheritance, `@media`; shared computed styles | `js/src/css.js` |
| Flattener | The styled DOM as few native nodes, incrementally; operations or the faster paths | `js/src/render.js` |
| Transitions, animations, canvas | CSS transitions and `@keyframes`; the 2d context as a recorded program | `js/src/transitions.js`, `animations.js`, `canvas.js` |
| Engine | One per window: the page, the tree and the platform, the frame clock, commands and timers | `src/native_ui/engine.zig` |
| Node tree | Yoga layout, paint order, sticky, tables, scrolling, hit testing, stamped rows | `tree.zig`, `dom_stamp.zig` |
| Text sizes | Measured by the platform, cached on the node and by content | `text_measure_cache.zig` and each backend |
| Backends | One view draws the page; platform widgets for the fields | `gtk.zig`, `win32.zig`, `appkit.zig`, `uikit.zig`, `android.zig` with `OrielNative.kt` |
| Tools | The render bench, the same tree with and without a change, per-stage timings | `examples/render-bench`, `tools/flatten_diff`, `-Dnative_ui_prof` |

More detail on each, and on Android's and Apple's backends, is in [`docs/native-renderer.md`](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/blob/main/docs/native-renderer.md).

## One frame

```=html
