```

On Android the boundary between Zig and Kotlin is crossed a few times per change, not per element. Kotlin keeps a copy of each node's properties: as JSON when the page's operations change them, a run's new words alone when only text changes, and for stamped rows each style once with a small record per node, all in one batch. It receives the frames at once, as one packed array, after each layout, and is asked to measure a text only when Zig's caches don't already know its size. Touches come back as taps, drags with fling and long presses, and are hit-tested in Zig on the node tree.

## Several elements, one native view

A browser keeps a box for every element. The flattener doesn't: most elements only group or style their children, and a native view per element would cost memory and time for nothing.

```=html
<figure class="nr-fig">
<svg viewBox="0 0 760 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="nr-flat-t">
  <title id="nr-flat-t">Flattening: a card's eight elements become six drawn nodes and one layout-only node</title>
  <defs><marker id="nr-arrow3" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 10 5 0 10z" class="nr-arrowhead"/></marker></defs>
  <rect class="nr-code" x="10" y="10" width="360" height="280" rx="10"/>
  <text class="nr-m" x="26" y="42">&lt;div class="card"&gt;</text>
  <text class="nr-m" x="42" y="70">&lt;h2&gt;Notes&lt;/h2&gt;</text>
  <text class="nr-m" x="42" y="98">&lt;p&gt;Kept in &lt;b&gt;SQLite&lt;/b&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</text>
  <text class="nr-m" x="42" y="126">&lt;div class="row"&gt;</text>
  <text class="nr-m" x="58" y="154">&lt;button&gt;</text>
  <text class="nr-m" x="74" y="182">&lt;svg&gt;…&lt;/svg&gt;</text>
  <text class="nr-m" x="74" y="210">&lt;span&gt;Add&lt;/span&gt;</text>
  <text class="nr-m" x="58" y="238">&lt;/button&gt;</text>
  <text class="nr-m" x="42" y="266">&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</text>
  <path class="nr-line" d="M380 150 H 404" marker-end="url(#nr-arrow3)"/>
  <rect class="nr-box nr-acc" x="410" y="10" width="340" height="280" rx="10"/>
  <text class="nr-t" x="426" y="40">View</text><text class="nr-s" x="470" y="40">background, radius, shadow</text>
  <text class="nr-t" x="442" y="70">Text</text><text class="nr-s" x="486" y="70">"Notes"</text>
  <text class="nr-t" x="442" y="98">Text</text><text class="nr-s" x="486" y="98">2 runs: "Kept in ", bold "SQLite"</text>
  <text class="nr-s nr-dim" x="442" y="126">.row: no view, only layout</text>
  <text class="nr-t" x="458" y="154">View</text><text class="nr-s" x="502" y="154">the button: clickable</text>
  <text class="nr-t" x="474" y="182">Icon</text><text class="nr-s" x="518" y="182">vector paths</text>
  <text class="nr-t" x="474" y="210">Text</text><text class="nr-s" x="518" y="210">"Add"</text>
  <text class="nr-s nr-dim" x="426" y="266">8 elements → 6 drawn nodes, 1 layout-only</text>
</svg>
</figure>
```

| HTML | Native |
|---|---|
| An element holding only text and inline elements (`b`, `i`, `a`, `span`, `code`, `br`…) | One text view with styled runs |
| A box that only lays out its children | A layout node only: nothing is drawn |
| A box with a background, border, radius or shadow | One drawn box |
| `input`, `textarea`, `select` | The platform's text field, text area or drop-down |
| Inline `svg` and `<use href="#symbol">` | One icon, drawn from its paths |
| `display: none`, `[hidden]` | Nothing |

Events go the other way: a tap on a node becomes a `click` on its element, bubbling through the DOM, with the browser's default actions (a link's `href="#…"`, a label's control, a form's submit button). Text fields send `input`, selects `change`, and Enter in a field reaches the page's `keydown` before it submits the form.

# What it supports

- **Selectors:** types, classes, ids, attributes, `:not()`, `:has()`, `:first-child`, `:last-child`, `:nth-child()`, `:focus`, `:focus-visible`, `:hover` (a mouse or trackpad), `:active` (while pressed), descendant and child combinators, `::before` and `::after` with `content`.
- **The cascade:** specificity, `!important`, inline styles, `var()` with fallbacks, `calc()`, `color-mix()`, inheritance (a `font-size` or `line-height` in `em` or `%` inherited as its computed length, as in browsers), `@media` on width, `prefers-color-scheme` and `pointer`.
- **Style sheets that change:** `<style>` and `<link>` added, removed, edited or disabled after the page loads, and the CSSOM (`document.styleSheets`, `insertRule`, `deleteRule`). CSS-in-JS works: styled-components and emotion apps look as in the WebView.
- **Layout:** `display` (`flex`, `block`, `inline-flex`, `none`; `grid` as wrapping rows), the flexbox properties, `gap`, sizes and their limits (in px, %, `calc()` mixing both, `max-content`, `fit-content`), `box-sizing`, margins (`auto` too), padding, `position: absolute`, `fixed` and `relative`, `inset`, `overflow` (scroll and clip, to the box's rounded corners too), `aspect-ratio`, transforms (`translate`, `scale`, `rotate`; scale and rotate are drawn, layout keeps the box).
- **Text:** fonts by `font-family` (the first installed family of the list, then the generic one: `system-ui`, `sans-serif`, `serif`, `monospace`; a page without one gets its WebView's default face), size, weight and italic; `line-height` from the font's metrics, mixed-font line boxes, baseline alignment, `letter-spacing`, `text-align`, `white-space`, `<br>`, underline and the spaces around inline elements. Inline text such as a `<code>` chip keeps its padding, border, radius and background across line fragments. Android measures fractional text widths with linear, subpixel metrics.
- **Drawing:** colors (hex, `rgb()`, `hsl()`), linear and radial gradients (including repeating gradients), borders (sides of different widths and colors joined as in browsers), `border-radius` (including elliptical corners on Linux, Windows and Apple), `box-shadow` (blurred like CSS), `opacity`, `outline` (with `outline-offset`, solid, dashed or dotted). SVGs honor their own `<style>` rules, media queries and presentation attributes; an SVG used as an image sees a light color scheme.
- **Forms and the keyboard:** inputs, text areas, selects, buttons, checkboxes and sliders as the platform's own controls, sized and lettered as its WebView draws them. Each field tells the page when it takes and loses focus (`focus`, `blur`, `focusin`, `focusout`), and its keys reach the page first (`keydown`, `keyup`; a page that prevents one keeps it from the field). Tab and Shift+Tab move the focus in the browser's order, with the platform browser's focus ring on `:focus-visible` (on macOS, as Safari: fields only unless Full Keyboard Access is on).
- **Pointer events:** `pointerdown`, `pointermove` and `pointerup` from a mouse, a pen or a touch, the touch events and the click that follow, `contextmenu`; moves are delivered once per display frame.
- **Editing and scrolling:** `beforeinput`, typed `input` events and selection synchronization on Windows, Android and Apple platforms; native text-area undo on Windows and macOS. Scroller offsets and `scroll` events are delivered at most once per display frame. Android controls use the WebView's default sizes. Unstyled macOS buttons use AppKit's default appearance.
- **Transitions:** `transition` (and its longhands) on opacity, backgrounds (colors and gradients), color, transforms, width and height, border colors and `box-shadow`, with durations, delays and timing functions (`ease`, `linear`, `cubic-bezier()`, `steps()`).
- **Animations:** `@keyframes` and `animation` (and its longhands): the same properties, iteration counts and `infinite`, directions (`alternate`, `reverse`), fill modes and delays. Frames that leave out `from` or `to` use the element's own values, as in CSS. While transitions or animations run, only the animated nodes are updated each frame: no styles or flattening.
- **More elements:** `<canvas>` with a 2d context (shapes, paths, gradients, text, transforms; drawn into a bitmap of its own, or from Zig with `oriel.canvas`), `<img>` (data: URIs, app assets and SVG files, `object-fit`), inline `<svg>` and `<use href>`, tables (`colspan`, `border-spacing`), `position: sticky`, horizontal scrolling (`overflow-x`).
- **JavaScript:** what DOM-based UI code uses: plain JavaScript, React, Vue, Svelte and Preact (see "Your web stack, unchanged"), ES modules, the app's commands through `window.oriel`, `URL` and `URLSearchParams`, timers, `localStorage`, `matchMedia`, `isContentEditable`.

Not yet: shadow DOM and constructed style sheets (`adoptedStyleSheets`, so web components such as Lit), animating `background-position`, `filter` and `backdrop-filter` (a blurred background is drawn opaque), skew and 3D transforms, grid areas and spans, floats, `iframe`, canvas `drawImage` and pixel access, `rowspan`, Vite's dev server (build the app; live development stays in the WebView). A page that measures text with `measureText` gets an estimate.

It is not a browser, and it isn't trying to be one: it runs the part of the web that an app's interface uses. When a page uses something outside it, the layout still works and the property is ignored.

# Status

| | Linux | Windows | macOS | iOS | Android |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Draws with | GTK 4, Cairo, Pango | Direct2D, DirectWrite | AppKit, Core Graphics, Core Text | UIKit, Core Graphics, Core Text | A Canvas view (Kotlin) |
| Fields | `GtkEntry`, `GtkTextView`, `GtkDropDown`, `GtkScale` | Edit, ComboBox, trackbar | `NSTextField`, `NSTextView`, `NSPopUpButton`, `NSSlider` | `UITextField`, `UITextView`, `UISlider` | `EditText`, `Spinner`, `SeekBar` |
| `<canvas>` | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |
| The showcase | Every tab | Every tab | Every tab | Every tab | Every tab |

Verified on a Linux desktop, a Windows 11 laptop, an M1 Mac, the iOS simulator, a Chromebook and a Pixel phone.

Next: accessibility (each node exposed to the screen reader), and grid areas. The design notes are in [`docs/native-renderer.md`](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/blob/main/docs/native-renderer.md) and [`docs/native-dom.md`](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/blob/main/docs/native-dom.md).
